<article class="partner-card pick-card">
          <object class="pick-photo" data="assets/images/brakecable.png" type="image/jpeg"
            aria-label="Coiled Brake Cable">
          </object>
          <div class="partner-body">
            <p class="tag">Towing Safety</p>
            <h2>Trailer Breakaway Cable,Car Accessories Heavy Duty Stainless Steel Spring Towing Coiled Wire,Travel
              Trailer Accessories,Suitable for Trailers,RVs,Mobile Homes,Emergency,Camper (4FT-1PCS)</h2>
            <aside aria-labelledby="breakaway-cable-take" class="pick-assessment">
              <h3 id="breakaway-cable-take">Jerry&#x2019;s take</h3>

              <p>
                That long thin RV break-away cable that triggers your RV brakes if you separate from the tow vehicle is
                a
                hugely important safety feature when towing your RV. However that cable
                tends to
                get in the way, dangle too low to the ground, tangle with the chains, etc. And it's gray color makes it
                easy to blend in with
                the pavement and accidentally get caught below your tongue jack pulling it out of the brake switch
                when raising the tongue. This item is an inexpensive solution. It stretches to the chain holes on your
                hitch.
                But it immediately retracts when you disconnect it.</p>
              <p><b><i>Some notes about replacing your existing cable:</i></b> The RV end of your cable 'snaps' in and
                out of
                the brake switch. I use quotes around 'snaps' because it may be in there very tight, and you might
                become
                convinced that you're damaging something by pulling hard on the cable to extract it. Trust me. That's
                what it's supposed to do.
                You will have to pull hard. Honestly, if comes out too easily I'd recommend a qualified technician take
                a look at it to prevent it accidentally coming out on a big bump in the road. So snap it out and snap
                the new one in. <b>
                  <font class="picks-inline-2">Warning</font>
                </b> Do not leave the clip out of the switch for a long period of time.
                Replace the clip immediately. While the clip is out, your emergency brakes on all RV wheels are engaged.
                Leaving them engaged for long periods of time could damage the brake motors or even burn them out. Plus
                it will drain your battery. A few minutes is fine. Hours... not so much.
              </p>
            </aside>

